Z||000010||fierospeeder||11-29-2000||04:42 PM||||i own a 87 tbird turbo,  the auto is 150 hp and the stickshift is 190hp, i heard because of lower boost and a different cam.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.designonesystems.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.designonesystems.com/</A> <BR>this is a website of a turbo for a 2.8 fiero<P>the link to a guy who put a 2.3l turbo on his fiero from a tbird doesn't have his page up anymore.  Im not sure if i have a text version of it, but he had pictures of a 2.3l turbo in his fiero.<P>the old link is this but it doesn't work anymore<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Track/7593/engine.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Track/7593/engine.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||PearlBlueSoul||11-29-2000||05:39 PM||||Ever tried a Turbo T/A?  I know they're rare as anything but that might be better suited.  I duno.  never seen one in person.  <P>and yap, I'd wager that's a near truck level turbo.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I remember from working at jiffy lube the turbos that were on the 7L powerstroke Diesels that came in.  hehe.  <P>I remember wanting one, but thinking "I could never POSSIBLY spool that. hehe.  you're a lucky lil basterd Cooter.  hehe   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">       <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.||||reg||1
Z||000012||BigTurbo87GT||11-29-2000||06:46 PM||||The turbo you have looks like a Holset H1C.<BR>I have one also, that I plan to you use on my 87 GT. They are used on Diesel engines displacing between 5 and 6 litres. Most notably the Dodge full size Cummins 5.9 L<BR>Based on some loose figuring I did, should be capable of getting 22 psi at 6K rpm.<BR>Should be around 4k rpm where you begin to accumulate noticable boost, and as mentioned previosly there will be a fair amount of lag but not terrible<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Formula||11-29-2000||07:09 PM||||1 horsepower = 746 watts<P><BR>so its for a 201 hp to 282 hp motor<P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Cooter||11-29-2000||07:15 PM||||22 psi? Wow! I was figuring on setting it to around 10. Thanks for all the input- keep the ideas coming.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Wannabe||11-30-2000||08:53 AM||||Like I said, unless you plan to build that engine for race only, it's too big. I've seen this over and over with turbos, headers, cams, carbs etc etc. People are always going too big and losing bottom end and driveablitiy! For the street, the 3.4 will do it's best work under 6000 rpm or even 5500. That gives you 1500-2000 rpm of useable power range. Sounds like a screaming deal though for $200. If it's worth over $2000, maybe you can try to re-sell it for  around $1000 and buy a turbo that will work properly   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Wannabe (edited 11-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Philphine||11-30-2000||11:44 AM||||speaking in ignorance. would it be possible to spool it with another smaller turbo? isn't that what they do on i think supras? sequential turbos?||||reg||1
Z||000017||JC Webb||11-30-2000||05:09 PM||||The Supra's smaller turbo does not feed or spool up the larger turbo.  It simply provides a small amount of boost at very low rpm until the larger turbo can take over.  An interesting concept worth trying on a Fiero!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Will||11-30-2000||07:04 PM||||Dennis Lagrua's webpage:<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/dlagrua/turbo.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/dlagrua/turbo.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000019||rogergarrison||12-01-2000||06:38 PM||||the turbo in mine is about the size  full roll of TP. i can get 15 pnds or so, but the can runs best with 4-5 pnds and NO LAG. any more boost is hard on engine components with not to much greater power, I can burn the tires off mine as is.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Phantom Rage||12-01-2000||09:42 PM||||Although not really too practical, except for perhaps drag racing, a shot of nitrous oxide can be used to spool up a large turbo.<P>I agree with Wannabe 100% on this one.  What are you building a racecar or a streetcar?  Ask yourself, what rpm range you usually drive within?  Be honest, for if you are not happy with your results you will only have yourself to kick for it.<P>Anyway, I hope you have a good time whatever the outcome <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||7
Z||000021||JimIndyRCR||12-02-2000||07:43 PM||||The most noteworthy aspect of this turbo (shown) is the split volute exhaust housing- very good design. If the exhaust is plumbed correctly -using three concerant cylinders to pulse each side of the exhaust turbine it will spool up much faster. Using this type of design we have seen up to 690 lbs of torque @ 2000 rpm with a small block chevy. it works. I too think this turbo is too big for a 3.4.<BR>Jim||||reg||1
Z||000022||RedOktober||12-04-2000||01:47 AM||||All I have to say is SWEET!!!!...<BR>My IHI Turbo from Miller Woods is smaller than the T-Bird's Turbo..(not by much). But since I got the IHI's compessor blades reclipped, I can build some serious boost.<BR>    Coooter.... You lucky bastard <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>     I would say GO FOR IT. Build your 3.4 right with the lower compression pistons, and a good o ringed head gasket. Just remember that the turbo is the easiest part. Plumbing, crossover pipe as well as mounting that monster will be the really fun part. BTW, dont forget the additional fuel you are going to need and the compuer to retard the ignition under full boost. and the list of other mods goes on and on ... but have fun.<BR>   If I can get 450Hp out of a 1.3 ltr rotory with twin turbos in a 93 RX-7(lots of time on the dyno), and my friends are getting a reliable 300 hp out of a Subaru 4 cyl, and 375 hp out of a honda CRX, you should be able to blast us all away. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <BR><P>------------------<BR>86 SE V6 Auto Turbo Going 3.4 OHV<BR>86 2M4 5sp (Beater)||||reg||1
Z||000023||Will||12-04-2000||11:25 AM||||Here's an idea, Cooter: forget the 3.4. Build a GN style 3.8 on a FWD block and use the big hair dryer on it. GN's are pushing better than 600 reliable HP out of their engines. The FWD block isn't as strong, but 500 HP shouldn't be out of reach.<BR>||||reg||1
